      "what_must_be_true": "The Industrial restock that produced Q2's 20% sequential jump and 56% YoY growth is still running after August 1, not a one-quarter pull-forward; AI data-center/ATE and aerospace continue to offset any auto or consumer softness; hybrid internal/external capacity actually ships the record bookings rather than creating a lead-time spike that later cancels; pricing stays resilient so incremental revenue drops through at ~49% adjusted operating margin; and the Q2 working-capital absorption reverses enough that FCF conversion stays above 30% of sales."
    },
    "preview_note": "ADI prints Q3 FY26 (ended August 1) against a $3.9B +/- $100M revenue box and $3.30 +/- $0.15 adjusted EPS. Q2 already cleared the high end at $3.62B / $3.09, with record B2B bookings and Industrial +20% QoQ / +56% YoY. The quarter is closed; this is a beat-or-miss on a completed period, not a live guide. Three things decide it. First, Industrial: management said automation, ETM, energy, healthcare and broad market were still below prior-cycle highs with lean channels — if that restock continued, revenue lands above $3.93B; a pause puts the print on the $3.8B rail. Second, mix and incremental margin: Q2 adjusted GM was 73% and adjusted OM 49%. A further Industrial/Communications-heavy mix should hold OM and push adjusted EPS to ~$3.38; a Consumer/Auto mix shift or utilization giveback would clip the EPS beat. Third, cash conversion: Q2 CFO was only $872M after an $808M working-capital use. The stock will treat sub-$1.1B CFO as evidence the top-line surge was inventory, not end demand. We expect a modest beat ($3.935B, $3.38 adj. EPS) and Q4 guide of $4.05B +/- $100M / $3.50 +/- $0.15. Reaction hinges on Industrial sequential growth and whether Q4 guide clears $4.0B.",
    "reasoning": "Latest reported quarter is Q2 FY26 (ended May 2, 2026), not the year-ago figures in the prompt template. Filed Q2 revenue was $3,623M, GAAP EPS $2.40, adjusted EPS $3.09, GAAP OM 38.1%, adjusted OM 49.0%, cash $2,437M. Company guided Q3 to $3.9B +/- $100M, GAAP EPS $2.60 +/- $0.15, adjusted EPS $3.30 +/- $0.15, ~39% GAAP / ~49% adjusted OM, 12–14% tax, $391M acquisition-related add-backs. I take the guide as the base but print above the midpoint: Q2 beat the high end, bookings were described as record across Industrial/Auto/Comms, Industrial was still below prior-cycle peaks with lean inventories, and ATE/data-center/A&D were called out as continuing into 2027. Sequential +8.6% to $3,935M is inside the guide band and below Q2's 15% sequential jump, so it does not require another surge. Operating leverage on that beat, with opex growing slower than sales and special charges near zero, produces GAAP NI $1,309M ($2.68) and non-GAAP NI $1,651M ($3.38) on 488.5M diluted shares. CFO rebounds from Q2's WC hole to $1,567M; capex -$145M; FCF $1,422M; buybacks and the $1.10 dividend keep CFF an outflow. BS rolls from the filed May 2 cash $2,436.92M and RE $11,526M. Q4 is modeled as a further mid-single-digit sequential step to $4,090M, which is also the midpoint I expect them to guide. FY26 sums Q1 $3,160 + Q2 $3,623 + Q3 $3,935 + Q4 $4,090. FY27–29 assume high-single-digit through-cycle growth as Industrial completes the restock and AI/automation/energy remain the secular stack, with FCF conversion staying in the mid-30s and almost all of it returned via buybacks and a slowly rising dividend."
  },
